\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The evolutionary baseline and the corpus move in opposite directions.} (a) The baseline's stationary distribution over the four canonical rules across the ten payoff scales, drawn as a stacked band. (b) The corpus's strategy mix on the same axis, drawn identically. (c) The AllD share alone, both accounts on one axis, with the gap shaded. {\color  {red}Panel (c) is a constructed contrast between two different objects, the stationary distribution of a process of selection among rules and the read-out labels of fixed agents in ten-round games; the shaded gap is descriptive and is not a test.} Baseline at $Z = 100$, $\beta = 0.1$, $\epsilon = 0.05$, ten rounds; the corpus pooled over five models, five languages and four persona pairings.}}{18}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:egtllm}{{6}{18}{\textbf {The evolutionary baseline and the corpus move in opposite directions.} (a) The baseline's stationary distribution over the four canonical rules across the ten payoff scales, drawn as a stacked band. (b) The corpus's strategy mix on the same axis, drawn identically. (c) The AllD share alone, both accounts on one axis, with the gap shaded. \rev {Panel (c) is a constructed contrast between two different objects, the stationary distribution of a process of selection among rules and the read-out labels of fixed agents in ten-round games; the shaded gap is descriptive and is not a test.} Baseline at $Z = 100$, $\beta = 0.1$, $\epsilon = 0.05$, ten rounds; the corpus pooled over five models, five languages and four persona pairings}{figure.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Own-action conditioning exceeds partner-action conditioning at the model level.} (a) Cells in the reciprocity by persistence plane, with the canonical rules at their exact coordinates; AllC, AllD and {\color  {red}Win-Stay-Lose-Shift} all have both main effects zero and so coincide at the origin. (b) The three contrasts per model, against the value each canonical rule would give, with 95\% percentile intervals from whole-dyad bootstrap resampling. (c) The first two principal components of the raw five-vector, which is given no knowledge of the contrasts and recovers the same geometry. Panels (a) and (c) use model-by-scale-by-language cells; a predecessor observed fewer than thirty times yields no estimate and the cell is dropped, leaving 212 of 250.}}{19}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:strategyspace}{{7}{19}{\textbf {Own-action conditioning exceeds partner-action conditioning at the model level.} (a) Cells in the reciprocity by persistence plane, with the canonical rules at their exact coordinates; AllC, AllD and \rev {Win-Stay-Lose-Shift} all have both main effects zero and so coincide at the origin. (b) The three contrasts per model, against the value each canonical rule would give, with 95\% percentile intervals from whole-dyad bootstrap resampling. (c) The first two principal components of the raw five-vector, which is given no knowledge of the contrasts and recovers the same geometry. Panels (a) and (c) use model-by-scale-by-language cells; a predecessor observed fewer than thirty times yields no estimate and the cell is dropped, leaving 212 of 250}{figure.caption.13}{}}
